text:
sam: customer reports intermittent SSE truncation and rapid client reconnects when behind their managed nginx (they see the final chunk missing ~20-30% of the time). logs show 200 then client RST. repro on our hosted API + their nginx config; attached minimal trace below.

jen: thanks — can you paste the nginx upstream config they're using? common culprits: proxy_buffering, proxy_request_buffering, and X-Accel-Buffering.

sam: they sent this snippet: ```
proxy_pass http://redwood-origin;
proxy_http_version 1.1;
proxy_set_header Connection "";
proxy_buffering on;
proxy_buffers 8 16k;
proxy_busy_buffers_size 32k;
proxy_request_buffering off;
```

alex: that proxy_set_header Connection "" is fine for h1 chunked. buffer sizes could still cause coalescing. nginx will buffer a response until buffer fills or upstream signals flush; with SSE/chunked streams you want buffering off or X-Accel-Buffering: no. Also check keepalive between nginx and upstream — half-close timing can produce a truncated final chunk if nginx decides to close connection before client reads final bytes.

khan: what TCP stack behavior are they seeing? do we have pcap/tcpmetrics? the RST suggests client gave up or nginx force-closed. we saw a few of these earlier when ALB idle probes raced with nginx keepalive_timeout.

sam: customer attached curl trace from client side: ```
curl -v -N https://api.redwood.ai/v1/stream -H "Authorization: Bearer ***"
> GET /v1/stream HTTP/1.1
< HTTP/1.1 200 OK
< Transfer-Encoding: chunked
< Content-Type: text/event-stream
...data chunks...
< 0
* Connection #0 to host api.redwood.ai left intact
```
They say sometimes the last data event never arrives and client reconnects.

jen: quick repro I ran against their nginx config (local): enabling proxy_buffering on + proxy_request_buffering off + small proxy_buffers reproduces a delayed final chunk where nginx aggregates and then closes. Try forcing: `proxy_buffering off; proxy_ignore_client_abort on;` or set header `X-Accel-Buffering: no` from upstream. Also ensure `sendfile off; tcp_nodelay on;`

alex: also add `proxy_set_header Connection "keep-alive";` and keepalive settings between nginx and upstream: `keepalive 16;` on the upstream block and `keepalive_timeout 75;` so nginx doesn't half-close mid-chunk. For HTTP/1.1 chunked streaming, `proxy_http_version 1.1` + `proxy_request_buffering off` is correct.

khan: operational note: ALBs can send idle probes (health / tcp) that reset connections; if client reconnects quickly we get billing churn. Customer mentioned they front nginx with a managed L7 that injects aggressive idle timeouts. can we recommend a client-side exponential backoff + resume? Also we should add a doc snippet about headers they can set.

sam: I drafted repro steps for them to validate the workaround: 1) set `proxy_buffering off; proxy_request_buffering off;` 2) add `proxy_set_header X-Accel-Buffering "no";` (or set from app) 3) ensure `proxy_http_version 1.1` and `proxy_set_header Connection ""` (empty) for chunked. Then `curl -v -N` and watch final chunk consistently.

jen: can we provide a minimal nginx upstream example to paste? I tested this locally and it fixed truncation every time: ```
upstream redwood-origin {
  server 10.0.0.5:8080;
  keepalive 16;
}
server {
  location /v1/stream {
    proxy_pass http://redwood-origin;
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Connection "";
    proxy_buffering off;
    proxy_request_buffering off;
    proxy_set_header X-Accel-Buffering "no";
    sendfile off;
    tcp_nodelay on;
  }
}
```

alex: addendum: if they must run proxy_buffering on for other routes, create a dedicated streaming location and avoid buffering there. mixing can be risky.

khan: for our internal tracking: flag this pattern as "nginx buffering + LB idle probe" under support KB. It crops up with managed nginx fronting private endpoints where buffer sizes are small. I'll make a short playbook.

sam: customer tried the minimal change and reports immediate improvement — final chunks are now consistent for 48 hours on their canary. they asked about a workaround if they can't change nginx. my suggestion: return an application-level keepalive event every ~15s so client sees activity and won't trigger upstream idle resets; also add a final explicit EOF event and idempotency keys so client can safely resume.

jen: approved. I'll push a short KB entry with nginx snippet, curl repro, and app-side keepalive suggestion. tagging deploy-bot to add KB doc frontmatter.

deploy-bot: KB page created: /kb/streaming/nginx-proxy-buffering-playbook — draft, reviewers: jen, khan.

sam: closing for now, will reopen if customer sees recurrence. :thumbsup:
